{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2757 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8daf0cf5dde20683f761844ee3f765ea4f06907135d8c4144001fa2d4e99ac07" :edb/db-before-t 2756 :edb/db-after-t 2757 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536069 50 #inst "2026-10-09T14:14:50.488Z" 13194139536069 true] [17592186147371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186382099 13194139536069 true] [17592186147372 1020 #edb/ref 17592186382092 13194139536069 true] [17592186147373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186382106 13194139536069 true] [17592186147373 1020 #edb/ref 17592186382113 13194139536069 true] [17592186148078 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536069 true] [17592186148078 1100 "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 4y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $2m/s$, find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139536069 false] [17592186148078 1100 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $2\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139536069 true] [17592186148078 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2R\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=2R\\,⟹\\,R=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $1\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382092 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382093 13194139536069 true] [17592186382092 1089 "q-79494" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382092 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536069 true] [17592186382092 1100 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." 13194139536069 true] [17592186382092 1113 #uuid "dcd4b923-bea2-5168-a3b2-3077cdbbb018" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382092 1145 "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9R\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=9R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382094 13194139536069 true] [17592186382093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382095 13194139536069 true] [17592186382093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382096 13194139536069 true] [17592186382093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382097 13194139536069 true] [17592186382093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382098 13194139536069 true] [17592186382093 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536069 true] [17592186382093 1041 "selection" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382093 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382096 13194139536069 true] [17592186382093 1110 #uuid "155056e4-6b01-569c-9f98-173a1e551d36" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382094 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m}" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382094 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382094 1111 #uuid "98062e33-0dea-51f2-96db-92c1aa49b6a5" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382095 1034 "\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{m}" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382095 1111 #uuid "9a6bb57e-e80d-579f-a04f-713b9a99a3a0" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382096 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382096 1111 #uuid "da9558f3-4792-56ac-9f97-33600fcb73ea" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382097 1034 "3\\,\\text{m}" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382097 1111 #uuid "1374676a-8d6e-5aa5-8305-21473638d1c7" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382098 1034 "\\frac{2}{9}\\,\\text{m}" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382098 1111 #uuid "da8d6bb4-728f-5cd9-8269-66e598d37822" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382099 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382100 13194139536069 true] [17592186382099 1089 "q-94275" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382099 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536069 true] [17592186382099 1100 "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." 13194139536069 true] [17592186382099 1113 #uuid "ff5f471e-8a86-5bf8-982b-2ec38a9cfd12" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382099 1145 "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382101 13194139536069 true] [17592186382100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382102 13194139536069 true] [17592186382100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382103 13194139536069 true] [17592186382100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382104 13194139536069 true] [17592186382100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382105 13194139536069 true] [17592186382100 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536069 true] [17592186382100 1041 "selection" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382100 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382103 13194139536069 true] [17592186382100 1110 #uuid "aa1cf199-0396-5cda-aee3-5066f446bb2f" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382101 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382101 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382101 1111 #uuid "75466738-745c-5eb1-a2ef-f5ca9e1c3915" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382102 1034 "I and III only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382102 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382102 1111 #uuid "2df1b856-55d8-52ce-a1a1-1f87a657c8ab" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382103 1034 "III only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382103 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382103 1111 #uuid "4f96029f-2cb7-5400-bf61-9b113149ed9c" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382104 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382104 1111 #uuid "3b9435ae-b2f1-5774-a3a2-0d1e42ec7c4e" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382105 1034 "II only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382105 1111 #uuid "17be7e3a-a5d6-525e-b2df-f105798b2da8" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382106 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382107 13194139536069 true] [17592186382106 1089 "q-94286" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382106 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536069 true] [17592186382106 1100 "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382106 1113 #uuid "ccb94392-acad-518a-b317-b59ffe1a3a81" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382106 1145 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{18}{3}=6.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=0.\n$$" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382108 13194139536069 true] [17592186382107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382109 13194139536069 true] [17592186382107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382110 13194139536069 true] [17592186382107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382111 13194139536069 true] [17592186382107 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382112 13194139536069 true] [17592186382107 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536069 true] [17592186382107 1041 "selection" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382107 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382110 13194139536069 true] [17592186382107 1110 #uuid "fa783c74-d3a9-5a57-a529-880c8bfab050" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382108 1034 "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{6}y=0" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382108 1111 #uuid "e2838c98-1d1f-51a7-9301-0b95c70e13d2" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382109 1034 "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{3}y=0" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382109 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382109 1111 #uuid "bb4712b3-9847-53df-9bbc-5b7d47195bb1" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382110 1034 "{y}^{″}+6y=0" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382110 1111 #uuid "c1f8097a-239f-5bdc-a054-5e5a695a7505" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382111 1034 "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382111 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382111 1111 #uuid "37ae2556-09e2-51b4-95c8-1d13ff0c7c4a" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382112 1034 "{y}^{″}+9y=0" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382112 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536069 true] [17592186382112 1111 #uuid "2ca27d28-8e16-5677-a575-fddb6d45e7d4" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382113 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382114 13194139536069 true] [17592186382113 1089 "q-94287" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382113 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536069 true] [17592186382113 1100 "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382113 1113 #uuid "934892af-2aea-587f-95fb-a008f0e0a602" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382113 1145 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{6}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =2$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139536069 true] [17592186382114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382115 13194139536069 true] [17592186382114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382116 13194139536069 true] [17592186382114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382117 13194139536069 true] [17592186382114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382118 13194139536069 true] [17592186382114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382119 13194139536069 true] [17592186382114 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536069 true] [17592186382114 1041 "selection" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382114 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382117 13194139536069 true] [17592186382114 1110 #uuid "e6d0598a-f5c3-5cf8-9157-36af83280b75" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382115 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382115 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382115 1111 #uuid "115cd997-b71b-55b2-87b1-321d1fc8e334" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382116 1034 "I and III only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382116 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382116 1111 #uuid "338b903c-62dd-5ae2-86ca-830414dfe7cd" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382117 1034 "III only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382117 1111 #uuid "9adf627e-e147-58a0-b5eb-ab1dbe8670cb" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382118 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382118 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382118 1111 #uuid "7bbadcd2-446b-58bb-a5dc-944482bffeb4" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382119 1034 "II only" 13194139536069 true] [17592186382119 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536069 true] [17592186382119 1111 #uuid "b5a7c864-ec43-5073-8381-13050f5d4620" 13194139536069 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-79494" 17592186382092 "q-79494/selection" 17592186382093 "q-79494/selection/17a585f7e449097654e6203a1cecfc345eb0add5b0dd21896ece23319aa2c53b" 17592186382094 "q-79494/selection/4fec99fc4a9141173b3054423d799014cbb82993957d0c6202f115942207fce7" 17592186382095 "q-79494/selection/6